WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.080
I want you to just take a second and think about a task that feels, you know, completely automatic to you right now.

00:06.160 --> 00:08.060
Like something that takes zero effort.

00:08.060 --> 00:13.940
Exactly. Just put yourself in that mental space. Maybe it is driving a car on your standard morning commute.

00:14.260 --> 00:15.120
Oh yeah, we've all been there.

00:15.300 --> 00:23.220
Right. You have probably had that experience where you pull into your driveway and you suddenly realize you do not actually remember the last ten minutes of the drive.

00:23.460 --> 00:25.000
It's a little scary when that happens, honestly.

00:25.080 --> 00:28.600
It really is. Or think about tying your shoelaces in the morning.

00:28.600 --> 00:32.360
Or even just reading the words on a billboard as you pass by it on the highway.

00:32.560 --> 00:34.300
Yeah, you don't even have to try to read the billboard.

00:34.500 --> 00:37.540
No, your brain just absorbs the meaning of the letters instantly.

00:37.800 --> 00:43.760
It feels like the world is just, well, presenting itself to you pre-translated. Like there is zero friction.

00:44.040 --> 00:45.700
Zero friction. Exactly.

00:46.340 --> 00:54.780
But if you rewind the tape of your life far enough, every single one of those tasks used to require this immense, almost agonizing level of concentration.

00:55.140 --> 00:55.640
Oh, absolutely.

00:55.640 --> 01:02.520
Think about when you were a toddler. You used to have to consciously think about every single loop, swoop, and pull of those shoelaces.

01:02.940 --> 01:03.860
It was a major event.

01:04.100 --> 01:11.940
Yeah. You used to have to sound out every individual syllable on a street sign, just staring at the letters until they finally formed a recognizable sound.

01:12.440 --> 01:15.660
So, I mean, the ultimate question for cognitive science has always been,

01:15.880 --> 01:21.840
what actually changed inside your head between being that struggling toddler and the effortless adult you are today?

01:21.840 --> 01:29.020
And for years, I mean, decades, really, the most popular answer to that question has been the dual process theory of the mind.

01:29.140 --> 01:29.380
Right.

01:29.620 --> 01:34.480
Which is arguably the most successful psychological framework ever exported to the general public.

01:34.480 --> 01:41.300
It's this incredibly famous, ubiquitous idea that our brains operate using two fundamentally different systems.

01:41.640 --> 01:42.880
System one and system two.

01:43.360 --> 01:43.720
Exactly.

01:44.080 --> 01:48.960
We have a fast, automatic, intuitive system, which we universally call system one.

01:49.420 --> 01:52.880
And we have a slow, deliberate, analytical system, system two.

01:52.880 --> 01:56.740
And this vocabulary has completely saturated our culture.

01:56.920 --> 01:57.400
Oh, it's everywhere.

01:57.800 --> 02:02.660
You see it in psychology textbooks, of course, but it has completely escaped the lab.

02:02.800 --> 02:06.240
You see it in corporate business seminars about decision making.

02:06.540 --> 02:06.660
Right.

02:06.900 --> 02:09.540
You see it in the way we talk about artificial intelligence.

02:10.140 --> 02:14.100
You hear it at dinner parties when someone says, oh, my system one just took over.

02:14.320 --> 02:15.600
Yeah. People use it constantly.

02:15.600 --> 02:25.160
It was popularized most famously by Daniel Kahneman in the early 2000s, and it gives us this very neat, very binary way to categorize the human experience.

02:25.700 --> 02:33.240
It basically tells us we essentially have two different machines living inside our skulls, and they trade off depending on what the situation demands.

02:33.580 --> 02:37.080
And I think it's everywhere because it feels so phenomenally right intuitively.

02:37.460 --> 02:38.220
It really does.

02:38.520 --> 02:42.240
We all experience certain thoughts as these effortless flashes of insight.

02:42.240 --> 02:48.020
You know instantly that someone's face looks angry, or you instantly know that two plus two is four.

02:48.560 --> 02:49.180
Without even trying.

02:49.480 --> 02:49.660
Right.

02:50.240 --> 02:55.480
And then on the flip side, we all experience other thoughts as this hard, grinding mental labor.

02:55.940 --> 02:59.940
Like trying to park a rental car in a tight space in a foreign country?

03:00.220 --> 03:00.500
Yes.

03:00.940 --> 03:03.460
Or calculating 17 times 24 in your head.

03:03.700 --> 03:03.980
Ugh.

03:04.300 --> 03:04.460
Yeah.

03:04.460 --> 03:14.720
And because the subjective feeling of those two experiences is so drastically different, well, it is incredibly natural to assume they must be coming from two entirely different cognitive architectures.

03:15.260 --> 03:15.500
Okay.

03:15.800 --> 03:16.620
Let's unpack this.

03:16.820 --> 03:21.440
Because what if that entirely binary categorization is completely wrong?

03:21.660 --> 03:22.780
Which is a wild thought.

03:22.980 --> 03:23.400
It is.

03:23.660 --> 03:28.660
What if the idea of having a fast mind and a slow mind is just a massive scientific misunderstanding?

03:29.300 --> 03:30.520
And that brings us to today.

03:30.860 --> 03:31.340
Exactly.

03:31.760 --> 03:33.620
That is the mission of our deep dive today.

03:33.620 --> 03:40.000
We are looking at a truly fascinating 2026 paper by an independent researcher operating under the name Flyxion.

03:40.640 --> 03:40.760
Right.

03:41.100 --> 03:46.020
The paper is titled Amortized Deliberation, Practice, and the Dual Process Partition.

03:46.980 --> 03:56.800
Our objective today is to explore this researcher's deeply mathematical, highly rigorous argument that System 1 and System 2 do not actually exist as separate cognitive machines.

03:56.960 --> 03:57.880
They don't exist at all.

03:57.880 --> 04:00.420
The paper argues that they are total illusions.

04:00.760 --> 04:03.860
They are just mirages created by our own history of practice.

04:04.240 --> 04:09.380
And to really get into the mechanics of this, I see our audio-visual backdrop has shifted to reflect the theme.

04:10.080 --> 04:10.420
Oh.

04:11.120 --> 04:11.920
Yeah, it has.

04:12.100 --> 04:13.900
We are no longer looking at a cozy library.

04:14.740 --> 04:18.900
The walls have basically stripped away to reveal a very minimalist, stark laboratory setting.

04:18.900 --> 04:20.080
It's very clean.

04:20.280 --> 04:20.780
Very clean.

04:21.180 --> 04:27.440
And the air around us is filled with these evolving, glowing mathematical graphs and practice curves plotting themselves in real time.

04:27.960 --> 04:28.960
It looks incredible.

04:29.500 --> 04:32.680
It is the perfect visual representation for what we are about to do.

04:32.960 --> 04:37.500
Because we are breaking down human cognition into some very observable, very hard data today.

04:37.500 --> 04:43.640
We have to strip away the poetic metaphors of two minds and look at the actual mathematical curves of practice.

04:44.620 --> 04:45.440
I love the backdrop.

04:46.000 --> 04:49.340
It feels like we are inside a giant data visualization.

04:49.860 --> 04:50.460
It really does.

04:50.980 --> 04:51.960
And that is necessary.

04:52.160 --> 04:57.660
Because the fundamental problem the author points out right away is how we define a task by how hard it is.

04:57.740 --> 04:57.940
Right.

04:58.020 --> 04:58.920
The intrinsic difficulty.

04:59.180 --> 04:59.360
Yeah.

04:59.700 --> 05:05.580
The standard assumption is that some tasks are just inherently System 1 tasks and some are inherently System 2 tasks.

05:05.580 --> 05:10.540
But this paper argues that speed and effort are not properties of a task itself.

05:10.880 --> 05:11.440
No, not at all.

05:11.800 --> 05:14.820
You cannot just point to an activity and say that is a fast activity.

05:15.380 --> 05:17.580
Instead, speed and effort are joint properties.

05:18.420 --> 05:19.660
They only exist in combination.

05:20.040 --> 05:20.360
Exactly.

05:20.780 --> 05:24.880
In the combination of a task and a person's specific history of practice.

05:25.400 --> 05:33.240
This is where the author uses a brilliant piece of formal logic to completely dismantle the basic premise of how cognitive scientists have categorized human thought for decades.

05:33.640 --> 05:35.040
The reversal pair, right?

05:35.040 --> 05:35.480
Yes.

05:35.880 --> 05:37.640
It relies on something called a reversal pair.

05:38.400 --> 05:39.700
Let me lay out the logic here.

05:40.300 --> 05:50.880
A partition claim, which is what the dual process theory relies on, basically says we can sort all human tasks into a fast bucket and a slow bucket, regardless of who is actually doing them.

05:51.020 --> 05:53.940
So the task itself is the thing that is either fast or slow.

05:53.940 --> 05:54.260
Right.

05:54.260 --> 06:05.740
But if we can find two tasks where the difficulty completely flips depending purely on the person's prior experience, well, then we can no longer claim that either task is intrinsically System 1 or System 2.

06:06.000 --> 06:13.120
Because if the difficulty is totally dependent on who is sitting in the chair, then the difficulty is not baked into the task itself.

06:13.120 --> 06:14.120
Precisely.

06:14.560 --> 06:16.340
The difficulty is not in the task.

06:16.440 --> 06:22.700
It is in the relationship between the geometry of the task and the historical rehearsal of the agent performing it.

06:23.440 --> 06:23.760
Okay.

06:24.040 --> 06:24.860
I follow that.

06:24.860 --> 06:35.940
So if task A is fast for you, but slow for me, and task B is slow for you, but fast for me, then neither task is intrinsically fast or slow.

06:35.940 --> 06:45.220
And the paper uses this incredibly relatable everyday example to prove this point, which completely blew my mind because it is so obvious once you see it.

06:45.460 --> 06:46.000
The keypads.

06:46.440 --> 06:46.700
Yes.

06:47.400 --> 06:48.860
Think about the layout of a keypad.

06:49.220 --> 06:55.740
If you pull out a standard telephone or open the dial pad on your smartphone, the numbers 1, 2, and 3 are on the top row.

06:55.900 --> 06:56.120
Right.

06:56.600 --> 07:00.420
The numbers 4, 5, and 6 are in the middle, and 7, 8, and 9 are on the bottom.

07:00.420 --> 07:02.820
But now, picture a calculator.

07:03.640 --> 07:07.280
Or the numeric keypad on the far right side of a standard computer keyboard.

07:07.620 --> 07:08.200
It's inverted.

07:08.520 --> 07:09.820
The layout is completely inverted.

07:09.960 --> 07:12.220
The numbers 7, 8, and 9 are on the top row.

07:12.520 --> 07:14.000
It is a perfect structural mirror.

07:14.380 --> 07:14.620
Yeah.

07:15.080 --> 07:20.980
And from a purely functional cognitive standpoint, operating these two keypads represents an identical irreducible demand.

07:21.500 --> 07:23.780
Meaning it's the exact same amount of work.

07:23.960 --> 07:24.320
Exactly.

07:24.820 --> 07:28.800
You are pressing four specific buttons in a sequence on a 3x3 grid.

07:28.800 --> 07:36.080
The raw computational requirement of moving your finger to a specific spatial coordinate is exactly the same.

07:36.680 --> 07:36.780
Right.

07:37.460 --> 07:44.600
So, imagine a task where you have to enter a specific four-digit code, let's say 4826, as rapidly as possible.

07:44.820 --> 07:45.060
Okay.

07:45.420 --> 07:52.340
Now, let us bring in a hypothetical person whose history involves working a job where they dial phone numbers all day long.

07:52.480 --> 07:54.760
Maybe a receptionist or telemarketer.

07:55.140 --> 07:56.660
Someone who dials constantly.

07:56.880 --> 07:58.500
But they never touch a calculator.

07:58.800 --> 08:03.960
For them, entering that code on the telephone layout is going to be incredibly fast.

08:04.080 --> 08:04.700
Oh, a blur.

08:05.040 --> 08:07.660
It is going to require almost zero conscious effort.

08:07.820 --> 08:09.040
They won't even look at the keys.

08:09.580 --> 08:13.780
It will look to an outside observer exactly like a classic System 1 process.

08:14.060 --> 08:16.240
It is a blur of automaticity.

08:16.240 --> 08:23.380
But if you take that exact same person and you hand them the calculator layout, suddenly the whole process halts.

08:23.700 --> 08:24.640
Everything slows down.

08:24.960 --> 08:25.780
They have to stop.

08:26.200 --> 08:28.380
They have to visually search the grid for the numbers.

08:28.860 --> 08:35.040
They have to consciously, deliberately guide their index finger to the 7, then the 8, then the 9.

08:35.040 --> 08:36.560
It's painful to watch sometimes.

08:36.760 --> 08:36.920
Right.

08:37.020 --> 08:39.840
The execution becomes incredibly slow and error prone.

08:40.260 --> 08:43.500
It looks exactly like a heavy grinding System 2 process.

08:43.500 --> 08:45.020
And here's the kicker.

08:45.020 --> 08:55.920
If you take an accountant, someone who uses a 10 key calculator layout all day long doing taxes, but who rarely ever makes manual phone calls, you get the exact reverse result.

08:56.160 --> 08:57.240
The perfect reversal.

08:57.560 --> 09:02.440
For the accountant, the phone layout becomes the slow, agonizing System 2 task.

09:02.440 --> 09:05.740
They are hunting and pecking on their iPhone screen.

09:05.940 --> 09:06.040
Yep.

09:06.520 --> 09:10.520
But the calculator layout, that is a blazing fast blur of muscle memory.

09:11.060 --> 09:13.000
The cost order completely flips.

09:13.220 --> 09:17.620
And it completely shatters the idea of a history independent cost separation.

09:18.080 --> 09:18.640
It really does.

09:19.060 --> 09:27.240
The dual process theory often treats the endpoints of a practice curve as if they are the outputs of two entirely different architectural machines in the brain.

09:27.340 --> 09:29.020
Like literally two different mechanisms.

09:29.380 --> 09:29.680
Yes.

09:29.680 --> 09:35.840
It says the slow hunt and peck is your analytical machine and the fast typing is your intuitive machine.

09:36.180 --> 09:44.440
But the keypad example proves that it is the exact same brain engaging with the exact same functional geometry, just with a different history of rehearsal.

09:45.300 --> 09:50.460
You do not swap out your System 2 brain for a System 1 brain when you move from the phone to the calculator.

09:50.700 --> 09:51.080
Exactly.

09:51.560 --> 09:54.880
You just encounter a mismatch with your stored physical habits.

09:55.120 --> 09:59.440
And once you see this, you start seeing these reversal pairs everywhere in daily life.

09:59.440 --> 10:00.080
They are everywhere.

10:00.360 --> 10:03.320
Think about a door that you have to push versus a door you have to pull.

10:03.560 --> 10:08.400
The famous Norman doors that have a handle implying you should pull, but the mechanics require a push.

10:08.500 --> 10:09.160
Oh, those are the worst.

10:09.200 --> 10:09.400
Right.

10:10.020 --> 10:14.840
It forces you into a slow, deliberate mode because it violates your history of how handles work.

10:14.940 --> 10:15.160
Yes.

10:15.160 --> 10:22.260
Or think about trying to use a friend's kitchen appliance where the dials are laid out completely differently than the one you have used at home for 10 years.

10:22.420 --> 10:23.160
You feel so clumsy.

10:23.340 --> 10:23.500
Yeah.

10:24.220 --> 10:30.880
Or on a much deeper cognitive level, think about speaking a language where the syntax and word order is different from your native tongue.

10:31.060 --> 10:31.800
That's a great example.

10:31.800 --> 10:36.300
A native English speaker has a terrible time putting the verb at the end of a sentence like they do in German.

10:36.480 --> 10:39.120
It feels like agonizing System 2 work.

10:39.860 --> 10:42.440
But to a native German speaker, it is completely automatic.

10:42.660 --> 10:43.680
System 1 fluency.

10:44.140 --> 10:57.100
In every single one of these cases, the formal logical proof demonstrates that any scientific theory that tries to permanently separate the easy tasks from the hard tasks will inevitably fail if the real world includes these reversal pairs.

10:57.100 --> 10:57.780
You just can't do it.

10:57.980 --> 10:58.160
No.

10:58.460 --> 11:05.680
You cannot put task A permanently in a fast category because someone else's life experience makes it their slowest task.

11:06.000 --> 11:09.420
I have to admit, I see a potential flaw in this logic, though.

11:09.540 --> 11:09.720
Oh.

11:10.100 --> 11:10.300
Yeah.

11:10.420 --> 11:12.180
I am going to push back on this a little bit.

11:12.400 --> 11:12.780
Let's hear it.

11:12.780 --> 11:17.720
I hear what you were saying about the keypads in the languages because those tasks are symmetrical.

11:18.000 --> 11:20.340
They are structurally identical, just flipped.

11:20.820 --> 11:20.960
Okay.

11:21.500 --> 11:26.180
But aren't there just some things that are universally objectively harder for a human brain to compute?

11:26.180 --> 11:29.440
Give me an example of what you mean by universally harder.

11:29.840 --> 11:30.740
Well, let's take math.

11:31.840 --> 11:38.360
Asking someone to solve a complex, multivariable calculus equation in their head versus asking them to add 2 plus 2.

11:38.580 --> 11:38.840
Right.

11:39.200 --> 11:41.000
It doesn't matter what your history is.

11:41.460 --> 11:44.420
The calculus is always going to demand more cognitive resources.

11:45.180 --> 11:45.460
Okay.

11:45.600 --> 11:46.180
I see where you're going.

11:46.860 --> 11:49.000
The analogy I think of is lifting weights at the gym.

11:49.840 --> 11:53.320
A 100-pound dumbbell is universally harder to lift than a 5-pound dumbbell.

11:53.320 --> 11:58.180
Well, regardless of whether you are a professional bodybuilder or someone who has never worked out a day in their life.

11:58.340 --> 11:58.620
That's true.

11:58.940 --> 11:59.200
Yes.

11:59.480 --> 12:03.220
The bodybuilder can lift the 100 pounds easily, and the beginner cannot even budget.

12:03.740 --> 12:06.880
But the 100-pound weight is always intrinsically heavier.

12:07.200 --> 12:09.140
The raw physical demand is higher.

12:09.800 --> 12:10.120
Right.

12:10.120 --> 12:20.240
Doesn't that imply that some cognitive tasks, like calculus, just naturally force you into a heavier System 2 mode of operation because the raw computational weight is higher?

12:20.900 --> 12:22.580
That is an excellent challenge, actually.

12:23.040 --> 12:27.960
And the weightlifting analogy highlights exactly the trap the old dual process theory falls into.

12:28.540 --> 12:28.760
Okay.

12:28.780 --> 12:29.140
How so?

12:29.140 --> 12:37.640
Because if we look closely at the paper's argument, the author is not actually denying that some computations require more raw steps than others.

12:38.140 --> 12:38.740
Oh, they aren't?

12:38.920 --> 12:39.080
No.

12:39.620 --> 12:44.620
The paper is not saying all tasks require the exact same absolute effort.

12:45.180 --> 12:48.120
A 1,000-piece puzzle takes more absolute moves than a 10-piece puzzle.

12:48.380 --> 12:49.140
That's just a fact.

12:49.520 --> 12:49.780
Right.

12:49.780 --> 12:54.520
What the text is clarifying here is the foundation of the categorization itself.

12:55.480 --> 13:00.780
The target of this critique is the scientific practice of inferring a biological partition from observed speed.

13:01.600 --> 13:04.000
Wait, let me make sure I am mapping this to reality.

13:04.600 --> 13:07.840
You are saying the problem isn't that calculus is hard.

13:08.040 --> 13:10.360
The problem is how scientists label it.

13:10.500 --> 13:11.020
Precisely.

13:11.020 --> 13:25.420
If a cognitive scientist is categorizing tasks into System 1 and System 2 purely based on the fact that one group of people performs one task fast and another task slow, their categorization is logically invalid.

13:25.800 --> 13:27.320
Because speed is the wrong metric.

13:27.600 --> 13:32.560
Because speed as a metric is hopelessly contaminated by the agent's practice history.

13:32.780 --> 13:33.840
Oh, I see.

13:33.840 --> 13:43.200
Even with your calculus example, a professional mathematician who has spent 20 years solving a specific type of complex equation might actually intuit the shape of the answer.

13:43.320 --> 13:50.140
They might literally see the solution in their mind's eye faster than a distracted, tired child can confidently add 2 plus 2.

13:50.340 --> 13:50.740
Wow.

13:50.920 --> 13:51.220
Okay.

13:51.560 --> 13:57.740
The mathematician has cached those specific structural patterns over decades of intense, focused rehearsal.

13:57.740 --> 14:04.700
So, even the 100-pound weight feels like 5 pounds if you have the right neural architecture built up to support it.

14:04.820 --> 14:05.180
Exactly.

14:06.120 --> 14:10.620
The paper acknowledges that there might be processes that are universally dependent on working memory.

14:11.140 --> 14:20.240
But it fiercely argues that you cannot use cost, meaning speed, latency, and perceived effort, as the primary sorting mechanism for brain architecture.

14:20.620 --> 14:22.760
Because it's too tied to the person's past?

14:22.760 --> 14:32.900
Because the moment you rely on speed to label something system 1, you are inherently measuring the agent's practice history, not just the task's intrinsic difficulty.

14:33.680 --> 14:39.380
Therefore, an intrinsic partition of tasks based on cost is a statistical impossibility.

14:40.140 --> 14:42.000
Okay, that actually makes perfect sense.

14:42.520 --> 14:45.380
The difficulty is a relationship between the task and the user.

14:45.600 --> 14:49.080
It is not an isolated property of the task sitting in a vacuum.

14:49.320 --> 14:49.560
Right.

14:49.560 --> 14:52.700
But this brings up a huge operational question.

14:53.060 --> 14:53.300
Which is?

14:53.980 --> 15:03.180
If we can't categorize tasks as being inherently fast or slow, and we are completely throwing out the idea of two different cognitive machines trading places inside our head,

15:03.800 --> 15:09.000
how exactly does a single brain get so blindingly fast at doing things?

15:09.380 --> 15:10.340
Ah, right.

15:10.680 --> 15:13.200
Because we know automaticity is a real phenomenon.

15:13.200 --> 15:17.260
I know for a fact I can tie my shoes while holding a conversation and looking at my phone.

15:17.260 --> 15:21.160
How does the one machine model actually achieve that level of speed?

15:21.680 --> 15:25.400
This is where we move from tearing down the old theory to building up the new one.

15:26.060 --> 15:30.440
And it revolves around the concept of practice as amortization.

15:31.220 --> 15:31.740
Amortization.

15:32.120 --> 15:32.640
Amortization.

15:32.760 --> 15:33.720
Like paying down a mortgage.

15:34.460 --> 15:35.040
Sort of, yeah.

15:35.700 --> 15:39.160
Amortization is a term usually used in finance or computer science.

15:39.940 --> 15:44.040
In finance, it means paying off a large initial cost gradually over time.

15:44.040 --> 15:55.620
In computer science, it means doing a really heavy, expensive computation once and then saving the result in a local cache so you don't ever have to pay that computational cost again.

15:56.180 --> 15:57.480
You just look up the saved answer.

15:57.740 --> 15:58.020
Yes.

15:58.780 --> 16:03.580
So the brain is essentially building up a massive library of pre-computed answers.

16:03.900 --> 16:04.200
Exactly.

16:04.200 --> 16:13.060
And to prove this, the paper relies on a very well-established empirical regularity in psychology known as the power law of practice.

16:13.700 --> 16:15.080
The power law of practice.

16:15.300 --> 16:15.560
Yes.

16:15.640 --> 16:24.100
This was established by researchers Newell and Rosenblum back in 1981 and further refined into the exponential law discussed by Heathcote and colleagues.

16:24.780 --> 16:26.900
These aren't new controversial findings.

16:27.060 --> 16:28.920
They are bedrock psychological data.

16:28.920 --> 16:35.520
And what do these mathematical laws actually show us about human performance when we practice something over and over?

16:35.940 --> 16:45.120
They show us that performance improvement, which we measure as the drop in the time it takes to do a task, follows a continuous mathematically smooth curve.

16:45.500 --> 16:47.240
A smooth curve.

16:47.500 --> 16:56.520
If you plot the learning curve of a human being doing a repetitive task, the mathematical formula basically shows a curve that just keeps sliding downward.

16:56.520 --> 17:04.940
It starts incredibly steep, meaning you get a lot faster in your first few attempts, and then it slowly flattens out as you approach your physical limit.

17:05.220 --> 17:05.460
Okay.

17:05.780 --> 17:09.520
But the crucial takeaway isn't the algebra itself, it's the shape of that curve.

17:09.900 --> 17:14.540
It is strictly decreasing, and most importantly, it is completely smooth.

17:15.140 --> 17:16.280
There are no sudden cliffs.

17:16.280 --> 17:26.040
Wait, so if the math is a perfectly smooth curve, that means picking the moment a task becomes automatic is basically completely arbitrary.

17:26.700 --> 17:27.460
Completely arbitrary.

17:27.880 --> 17:33.340
I'm just picking a random point on a downward slope and deciding, okay, now I'm using system one.

17:33.540 --> 17:33.920
Exactly.

17:34.460 --> 17:38.360
You just hit on what the author calls eradicating the magic threshold.

17:38.920 --> 17:40.920
The magic threshold.

17:41.260 --> 17:41.920
I like that.

17:41.920 --> 17:50.160
When people talk about dual process theory in popular culture, they often use language like, if you practice enough, eventually the task becomes automatic.

17:50.260 --> 17:51.060
Yeah, I've said that.

17:51.600 --> 18:03.900
They heavily imply there is a distinct moment, a specific point in time, or a specific number of repetitions, where the slow system two hands the steering wheel over to the fast system one.

18:04.460 --> 18:06.400
It implies a mechanical relay switch.

18:06.400 --> 18:09.000
Like shifting gears in a manual transmission car.

18:09.260 --> 18:15.420
You are in first gear, which is loud and slow, and then you hit the clutch and physically move into second gear.

18:15.600 --> 18:15.900
Right.

18:16.080 --> 18:17.760
But the math proves there is no clutch.

18:18.240 --> 18:19.280
There is no gear shift.

18:20.120 --> 18:26.700
For any arbitrary threshold of time you choose to define as automatic, the slope of the cost curve remains continuous.

18:27.060 --> 18:27.920
There's no jump.

18:28.220 --> 18:30.820
There is absolutely no sudden jump in the data.

18:31.040 --> 18:35.680
There is no step in the graph where one mechanism shuts off and another distinctly turns on.

18:36.400 --> 18:39.680
The threshold is an absolute invention of the observer.

18:40.320 --> 18:45.480
It is a convenient fiction, we tell ourselves, because human beings like to categorize things into neat boxes.

18:45.800 --> 18:56.100
So when I say I practiced playing this song on the guitar until it became automatic, I am basically just drawing a completely invisible made-up line in the sand.

18:56.400 --> 18:56.960
Precisely.

18:56.960 --> 19:12.640
A continuous mathematical function does not strictly forbid two mechanisms from operating simultaneously and changing their relative contributions over time, but it utterly destroys the evidence that crossing some magical threshold is proof of a categorical switch.

19:12.880 --> 19:13.140
Right.

19:13.660 --> 19:17.980
You cannot point to a smooth slope and claim it as evidence of two different staircases.

19:17.980 --> 19:19.940
That makes total sense.

19:20.500 --> 19:24.020
I love how this paper doesn't just stop at breaking down the old theory.

19:24.720 --> 19:27.000
It doesn't just say, you're all wrong, goodbye.

19:27.640 --> 19:28.960
No, it offers a replacement.

19:29.400 --> 19:29.600
Right.

19:29.960 --> 19:34.900
It actually provides a working, highly detailed mathematical model for the single process alternative.

19:35.400 --> 19:40.700
It explains how this smooth curve actually operates inside our neurobiology.

19:40.960 --> 19:41.220
Yes.

19:41.220 --> 19:43.740
The text calls this the memoization model.

19:44.160 --> 19:47.940
And this model is built on the shoulders of some giants in cognitive psychology.

19:48.220 --> 19:57.220
The paper draws heavily on Gordon Logan's instance theory, which is the idea that automaticity is just the retrieval of specific stored episodes from our past.

19:57.760 --> 20:00.380
And John Anderson's concept of knowledge compilation.

20:01.200 --> 20:02.440
Knowledge compilation.

20:02.860 --> 20:03.460
Yes.

20:03.700 --> 20:11.120
Which is about how our brains convert slow, interpretive, declarative knowledge into fast, direct procedural routines.

20:12.120 --> 20:19.280
The author synthesizes these concepts into a single, elegant equation for the expected cost of a cognitive task.

20:19.720 --> 20:21.440
So what does this all mean?

20:22.040 --> 20:31.700
I want to really dive deeply into this single process model because when I was reading the source material, I came up with an analogy that I think maps perfectly onto the variables in this equation.

20:32.060 --> 20:34.380
Oh, I am always a fan of a strong analogy.

20:34.940 --> 20:35.500
Let's hear it.

20:35.720 --> 20:40.400
I will track your analogy against the formal variables in the text's single process model.

20:40.400 --> 20:41.600
Okay, perfect.

20:42.200 --> 20:49.800
The model says that the total expected effort or cost of any task we face is a combination of several specific potential actions.

20:50.140 --> 20:50.340
Right.

20:50.620 --> 20:54.160
I like to think of navigating these actions like cooking a complex dinner in my kitchen.

20:54.620 --> 20:57.740
Let us say I want to make a really elaborate traditional lasagna.

20:58.080 --> 20:59.140
Okay, I'm tracking.

20:59.140 --> 21:04.200
Now, if I have never made this specific lasagna before, I have to solve the problem from scratch.

21:04.600 --> 21:12.980
I have to find the cookbook, read every single line of the recipe, carefully measure out exactly two cups of flour, precisely measure the ricotta.

21:13.400 --> 21:16.080
I have to read the instructions on how long to boil the noodles.

21:16.320 --> 21:16.960
It's a whole ordeal.

21:16.960 --> 21:17.720
It is.

21:17.980 --> 21:19.520
That process is incredibly slow.

21:19.860 --> 21:21.540
It is highly prone to error.

21:21.740 --> 21:23.920
And it takes a massive amount of mental effort.

21:24.740 --> 21:29.340
In the formal model, this is represented by the variable S, the solve from scratch cost.

21:29.580 --> 21:31.000
The solve from scratch cost.

21:31.160 --> 21:31.360
Yes.

21:31.860 --> 21:37.160
And just like in your kitchen, S is the most expensive, highest latency route the brain can possibly take.

21:37.540 --> 21:39.960
It is raw, unamortized computation.

21:40.180 --> 21:41.940
Okay, now let's fast forward a year.

21:42.060 --> 21:42.320
All right.

21:42.320 --> 21:46.280
I have made this exact same lasagna 50 times for my family.

21:46.580 --> 21:48.760
I have the recipe completely memorized.

21:49.280 --> 21:52.160
I have stored that specific episode in my long-term memory.

21:52.800 --> 21:56.700
According to the paper, retrieving that stored memory has a cost.

21:56.860 --> 21:57.240
It does.

21:57.380 --> 22:01.260
It is a very, very small cost compared to reading the cookbook, but it is not zero.

22:01.440 --> 22:01.740
Correct.

22:02.040 --> 22:03.980
The paper calls this the retrieval cost.

22:04.360 --> 22:12.300
It is the baseline computational expense of pinging your own memory banks to see if the stored solution to the current problem exists.

22:12.900 --> 22:15.800
Even a fast lookup requires some biological energy.

22:16.580 --> 22:20.280
But here is the crucial part that I think people miss when they talk about habits.

22:20.440 --> 22:20.720
What's that?

22:21.120 --> 22:27.380
Even if I instantly retrieve the perfectly memorized lasagna recipe from my brain, I cannot just blindly start cooking.

22:27.540 --> 22:30.500
I still have to walk over to my pantry and open the refrigerator.

22:30.880 --> 22:31.100
Right.

22:31.180 --> 22:32.200
You have to check your supplies.

22:32.200 --> 22:32.680
Exactly.

22:33.360 --> 22:39.600
I have to look inside and make sure I actually have ricotta cheese and ground beef and tomato sauce in the house today.

22:40.200 --> 22:43.100
The current environment has to perfectly match the memory.

22:43.740 --> 22:45.660
The paper calls this the assessment cost.

22:46.340 --> 22:50.240
This is a vital nuance, and I am glad your analogy highlights it.

22:50.780 --> 22:58.140
The assessment cost is absolutely critical because a stored answer in your brain was verified for the situation in which it was originally produced.

22:58.240 --> 22:59.300
The exact situation.

22:59.300 --> 22:59.860
Right.

23:00.260 --> 23:09.720
The text explicitly states that reuse of a memory is warranted only when the present instance preserves the exact conditions on which that original deliberate solution depended.

23:10.300 --> 23:11.840
You cannot blindly execute.

23:12.020 --> 23:15.420
You have to pay the assessment cost to check the environmental conditions.

23:15.420 --> 23:15.900
Exactly.

23:16.420 --> 23:23.300
Because if I look in the fridge and I realize I only have cottage cheese instead of ricotta, the exact conditions are broken.

23:23.620 --> 23:23.900
Ah.

23:24.260 --> 23:25.900
I have to stop and make a substitution.

23:26.420 --> 23:30.580
I have to mentally calculate how cottage cheese will change the moisture of the lasagna.

23:31.140 --> 23:35.240
I have to adapt my stored memory to fit the current, slightly altered reality.

23:35.880 --> 23:37.620
And that mental pivot has a cost, too.

23:37.900 --> 23:39.980
The model defines this dynamically.

23:39.980 --> 23:49.920
It takes the probability of applicability, meaning the odds that your memory mostly fits the situation, and multiplies it by the adaptation cost.

23:50.420 --> 23:50.580
Okay.

23:50.960 --> 23:53.040
So that covers substitutions.

23:53.540 --> 23:57.340
And what if I open the pantry and it is completely bare?

23:57.660 --> 24:03.320
If I have absolutely zero ingredients, meaning the memory completely fails to apply to the current reality.

24:03.640 --> 24:06.800
Then you suffer what the paper calls the fallback cost.

24:07.340 --> 24:08.160
The fallback cost.

24:08.160 --> 24:16.180
So I have to abandon the memory entirely, get my keys, drive to the grocery store, buy the ingredients, and basically start from scratch anyway.

24:16.520 --> 24:16.780
Yes.

24:17.240 --> 24:28.020
In this scenario, I have paid the small retrieval cost to remember the recipe, I have paid the assessment cost to check the pantry, and now I still have to pay the massive salt-from-scratch cost.

24:28.360 --> 24:31.180
Your kitchen analogy maps flawlessly to the formal equation.

24:31.600 --> 24:32.100
Oh, awesome.

24:32.100 --> 24:40.520
The brilliant thing about this single-process model is that the human agent does not magically know which route is going to be faster before they start the task.

24:41.040 --> 24:45.500
You always, inevitably, pay the retrieval and assessment overhead.

24:45.780 --> 24:47.100
You always check the pantry.

24:47.300 --> 24:48.520
You always check the pantry.

24:49.180 --> 24:53.180
If the stored case is a perfect match, you grab the ingredients and finish quickly.

24:53.340 --> 24:55.260
That looks like system one.

24:55.440 --> 24:55.700
Right.

24:55.700 --> 25:01.960
If it isn't a perfect match, you fall back to deliberate calculation that looks like system two.

25:02.860 --> 25:09.880
But both scenarios are happening within the exact same cognitive architecture, executing the exact same sequential procedure.

25:09.880 --> 25:16.800
And there is a really profound, almost philosophical point the author makes here about how we view human behavior.

25:17.300 --> 25:27.500
Just because you are cooking the lasagna rapidly from memory, just because you are executing a fast, effortless, habitual response, that does not mean you never evaluated the task.

25:28.020 --> 25:31.500
I think this is one of the most misunderstood aspects of human psychology.

25:31.780 --> 25:32.060
Really?

25:32.060 --> 25:33.020
Oh, absolutely.

25:33.540 --> 25:36.280
The text is fiercely explicit on this point.

25:36.820 --> 25:42.820
Fast execution is absolutely not evidence that a deliberate evaluation never occurred in the past.

25:43.060 --> 25:46.600
Right, because I did evaluate it a year ago when I read the cookbook.

25:46.820 --> 25:47.240
Exactly.

25:47.860 --> 25:54.840
When you watch a master pianist play a complex sonata effortlessly, or you watch a professional athlete react to a play in milliseconds,

25:54.840 --> 26:01.740
it is so tempting to say they are acting on blind intuition or relying on some primitive, non-conscious system.

26:01.880 --> 26:04.220
Yeah, we say they just have natural instinct.

26:04.700 --> 26:05.920
We strip them of their agency.

26:06.720 --> 26:19.000
But in reality, that fast response is just a heavily amortized, perfectly cached result of a very rigorous, deeply deliberate evaluation they executed hundreds of times in a practice room years ago.

26:19.180 --> 26:20.400
That makes a lot of sense.

26:20.400 --> 26:24.420
The exact conditions matched, the assessment cleared, and they executed.

26:24.860 --> 26:28.700
The deep evaluation is still there, it has just been shifted backward in time.

26:29.000 --> 26:31.100
It is frozen deliberation.

26:31.320 --> 26:32.100
I love that concept.

26:32.260 --> 26:33.180
It's very poetic.

26:33.700 --> 26:36.900
So, this single process equation makes total sense to me.

26:36.980 --> 26:45.420
It explains the smooth curve, it explains why we get faster, and it relies entirely on one unified brain just getting better at looking up its own past homework.

26:45.600 --> 26:45.840
Yes.

26:45.840 --> 26:54.640
But if we assume this is true, it raises a massive, glaring logistical problem for the people who still believe in the old dual process theory.

26:55.020 --> 27:01.520
This brings us to what the author calls the routing argument, and it feels like the fatal strike against System 1 and System 2.

27:01.800 --> 27:03.780
It is a devastating logical blow.

27:04.620 --> 27:08.560
Think about the physical mechanics required for a two-system brain to actually function.

27:09.080 --> 27:09.420
Okay.

27:09.420 --> 27:20.140
If you genuinely have two distinct cognitive machines, you must have some kind of mechanism that decides which machine to turn on for any given task.

27:20.480 --> 27:22.000
Right, like a switchboard operator.

27:22.340 --> 27:22.640
Yes.

27:22.800 --> 27:31.260
There must be a router or a dispatcher sitting up there in your cortex looking at an incoming problem, judging it, and saying,

27:31.260 --> 27:35.620
Ah, this is an easy face recognition problem. Send it to System 1.

27:36.180 --> 27:39.600
Or, uh-oh, this is hard math. Send this to System 2.

27:40.060 --> 27:45.300
Because if System 1 is fast, but it can't do math, something has to prevent it from trying and failing.

27:45.560 --> 27:45.920
Exactly.

27:46.760 --> 27:50.160
And the author formalizes this problem mathematically in the text.

27:50.160 --> 27:57.700
If you have two systems, the act of assigning a task to a system is itself a computational task.

27:58.540 --> 27:59.840
The dispatcher has to work, too.

28:00.080 --> 28:02.680
The dispatcher has to do work to make a decision.

28:03.500 --> 28:11.720
And the math proves that the temporal cost of routing the task must inherently be cheaper than the actual speed advantage you gain by using the fast system.

28:12.140 --> 28:18.540
Oh, because if the router is slow, it completely defeats the entire evolutionary purpose of having a fast system in the first place.

28:18.640 --> 28:18.880
Yes.

28:18.880 --> 28:30.360
If it takes my brain three seconds to decide whether to use my fast system or my slow system, and the fast system only saves me one second of processing time, I have suffered a massive net loss.

28:30.500 --> 28:31.880
I am slower overall.

28:32.520 --> 28:32.980
Precisely.

28:33.180 --> 28:38.220
The overhead cost of a complex dispatcher ruins the economics of the dual-process architecture.

28:38.580 --> 28:38.920
Wow.

28:39.560 --> 28:42.560
But the author's argument goes much deeper than just processing latency.

28:43.000 --> 28:45.720
It strikes at the very heart of cognitive philosophy.

28:45.720 --> 28:50.380
The author points out something called the fallacy of the little supervisor.

28:51.280 --> 28:52.980
The fallacy of the little supervisor.

28:53.520 --> 28:57.120
I have heard this referred to as the homunculus problem in philosophy.

28:57.340 --> 28:58.800
Rene Descartes dealt with this.

28:58.800 --> 29:00.900
It is the exact same trap.

29:01.380 --> 29:11.960
If you posit a dispatcher that surveys a thought, judges how complex it is, and then routes it to the appropriate cognitive machine, you haven't actually explained how human intelligence works at all.

29:12.180 --> 29:13.240
No, you really haven't.

29:13.240 --> 29:19.960
You have just invented a tiny, hyper-intelligent little person, a homunculus, living inside the brain.

29:20.300 --> 29:24.940
And you have left the intelligence of that tiny supervisor completely unexplained.

29:25.440 --> 29:25.960
Right.

29:25.960 --> 29:32.780
How does the dispatcher magically know how hard a calculus problem is without actually doing the calculus first?

29:33.260 --> 29:33.520
Exactly.

29:34.160 --> 29:37.500
To know that a problem is hard, you have to somewhat understand the problem.

29:37.920 --> 29:42.640
The whole point of dual-process theory was to explain how human beings make judgments.

29:43.040 --> 29:43.240
Yes.

29:43.880 --> 29:51.360
But if your theory requires a magical little judge to pre-sort the judgments before they are judged, I mean, you are trapped in an infinite loop.

29:51.440 --> 29:52.940
It is turtles all the way down.

29:53.040 --> 29:54.380
It is an infinite regress.

29:54.380 --> 29:59.700
You have outsourced the mystery of cognition to a smaller, unexplained box inside the brain.

30:00.500 --> 30:01.040
Wait, wait, wait.

30:01.440 --> 30:06.640
Let me play devil's advocate here because this feels almost too absolute.

30:06.960 --> 30:07.260
Okay.

30:07.580 --> 30:12.780
Is the paper saying that all routing in the brain is completely biologically impossible?

30:13.340 --> 30:13.980
No, not quite.

30:14.160 --> 30:18.920
Because I know from my own lived experience that we clearly do shift gears.

30:18.920 --> 30:24.820
Sometimes I am driving, acting on complete autopilot, and suddenly a deer jumps into the road.

30:24.980 --> 30:29.120
And I consciously feel my brain snap into a hyper-focused, deliberate mode.

30:29.240 --> 30:30.580
I am routing resources.

30:31.020 --> 30:32.740
That is a crucial distinction.

30:33.340 --> 30:36.000
And the paper is very careful not to overstep here.

30:36.320 --> 30:39.120
It is not saying that all routing is impossible.

30:39.120 --> 30:39.900
Okay, good.

30:40.440 --> 30:52.700
It is saying that a magical, categorical router, one that just inherently knows the difference between System 1 and System 2 without doing any work, is logically useless and scientifically indefensible.

30:52.920 --> 30:53.580
Ah, I see.

30:53.580 --> 30:58.260
A mechanical, explicable routing policy, however, is perfectly fine.

30:59.200 --> 31:00.780
And here is the beauty of it.

31:01.380 --> 31:12.140
If we look back at our single-process lasagna analogy, the retrieval and assessment steps, checking the memory and checking the pantry, that exact sequence is the router.

31:12.800 --> 31:13.100
Ah.

31:13.400 --> 31:17.800
Because checking the pantry naturally determines whether I go fast or go slow.

31:18.060 --> 31:18.560
Exactly.

31:18.560 --> 31:23.640
The crucial difference is that the cost of checking the pantry is paid for every single time, uniformly.

31:24.560 --> 31:25.920
There is no magical prejudgment.

31:26.260 --> 31:28.700
There is no magic homunculus making a prejudgment.

31:29.300 --> 31:30.060
You ping the memory.

31:30.400 --> 31:32.800
If the conditions match perfectly, you execute quickly.

31:33.500 --> 31:39.720
If the assessment fails, because the conditions changed, you are mechanically forced to fall back to the slow, deliberate route.

31:40.100 --> 31:41.480
That is so elegant.

31:41.480 --> 31:49.560
The success or failure of the fast route is entirely settled by mechanical, sequential steps that you already pay the biological cost to execute.

31:50.440 --> 31:53.800
So the single-process model inherently contains its own routing logic.

31:54.240 --> 31:58.480
It doesn't need a little supervisor because the structure of the memory lookup is the dispatcher.

31:59.340 --> 31:59.480
Precisely.

32:00.480 --> 32:10.160
The text also brings up a very serious, highly respected alternative theory from researchers Shenhaft, Bodvinik, and Cohen called the expected value of control account.

32:10.160 --> 32:12.360
The expected value of control.

32:12.660 --> 32:12.880
Yes.

32:13.160 --> 32:19.000
This is a framework where cognitive control is allocated based on a graded, continuous cost-benefit analysis.

32:19.940 --> 32:26.680
The brain constantly weighs the expected benefit of deploying intense focus against the heavy metabolic effort cost of doing so.

32:27.180 --> 32:28.000
Now that makes sense.

32:28.400 --> 32:31.560
That is a specified, mechanically testable mechanism.

32:32.120 --> 32:34.580
It is not an unexplained categorical sorter.

32:35.500 --> 32:37.520
A learned mechanical policy is defensible.

32:37.520 --> 32:44.940
But the casual assumption that a supervisor just knows what is system one and system two is a fatal flaw in the popular doctrine.

32:45.580 --> 32:52.500
Okay, so if the science is this robust, if the mathematical curves point to a single, continuous system of practice,

32:53.120 --> 32:58.360
and the routing logic of two systems requires a magical, tiny person living in our heads,

32:58.860 --> 33:03.020
why on earth are we also universally convinced that we have two separate minds?

33:03.160 --> 33:03.860
That's a great question.

33:03.860 --> 33:08.060
Why does it feel so undeniably real to every single one of us?

33:08.180 --> 33:11.920
Because human beings are remarkably susceptible to systematic illusions.

33:12.120 --> 33:13.560
We do trick ourselves a lot.

33:13.820 --> 33:14.140
We do.

33:14.820 --> 33:22.000
The author dedicates a massive portion of the paper to breaking down three specific, deeply ingrained errors in how we observe ourselves,

33:22.540 --> 33:25.300
and more importantly, how scientists conduct their studies.

33:25.720 --> 33:28.060
Oh, so the scientists are falling for it too.

33:28.060 --> 33:28.700
Absolutely.

33:29.340 --> 33:35.000
These errors combine to trick us into believing the system one and system two partition is an objective reality.

33:35.720 --> 33:37.920
The first one is called the error of selection.

33:38.340 --> 33:44.340
This one fascinated me because it exposes how cyclical and biased academic research can actually be.

33:44.520 --> 33:44.840
It does.

33:45.140 --> 33:49.020
The paper points out that when researchers want to study system one,

33:49.600 --> 33:54.060
they intentionally pick tasks that are inherently incredibly easy for adult humans.

33:54.060 --> 33:54.620
Yes.

33:54.880 --> 33:59.660
They pick things like recognizing a facial expression or reading a basic word.

34:00.320 --> 34:05.220
These are tasks where the number of prior practice episodes over a human lifetime is in the tens of millions,

34:05.420 --> 34:08.400
and they label those heavily practiced tasks system one.

34:08.860 --> 34:11.320
And then when they want to study system two,

34:11.660 --> 34:16.280
they intentionally select tasks that are extraordinarily rare or structurally novel.

34:16.540 --> 34:16.820
Right.

34:16.820 --> 34:21.060
They use complex logic puzzles or abstract probability questions,

34:21.420 --> 34:24.780
things the average person might encounter a handful of times in a decade.

34:25.520 --> 34:28.060
It is a massive self-fulfilling prophecy.

34:28.500 --> 34:29.420
It absolutely is.

34:29.840 --> 34:34.980
If you define system one by selecting tasks that people have practiced tens of thousands of times,

34:35.460 --> 34:39.340
and you define system two by selecting tasks they have zero practice with,

34:39.900 --> 34:44.180
of course you will find a massive, statistically significant difference in speed and latency.

34:44.700 --> 34:45.360
Of course you will.

34:45.360 --> 34:47.720
But as the text firmly states,

34:48.320 --> 34:54.500
finding that system one tasks are cheap to execute is merely a restatement of how the tasks were chosen in the first place.

34:54.760 --> 34:55.040
Wow.

34:55.660 --> 35:00.180
You are just observing the universally accepted effects of high practice versus low practice.

35:00.680 --> 35:03.940
You are not discovering the existence of two different brain architectures.

35:04.320 --> 35:08.340
You are just discovering that practice works, which we already knew.

35:08.500 --> 35:08.840
Exactly.

35:09.280 --> 35:11.560
The methodology guarantees the result.

35:11.560 --> 35:16.340
The second error the paper identifies is the error of salience.

35:16.480 --> 35:20.340
And this one is so fundamentally, beautifully human.

35:20.960 --> 35:25.340
The paper argues that our memory is heavily biased toward extremes.

35:25.900 --> 35:28.960
We only remember the dramatic endpoints of a learning curve.

35:28.960 --> 35:32.140
Think about the process of learning to play a musical instrument.

35:32.240 --> 35:33.220
Yes, the guitar.

35:33.640 --> 35:33.880
Right.

35:34.260 --> 35:39.060
When you first learn to play the guitar, you vividly remember that first terrible month.

35:39.600 --> 35:48.660
Your fingertips are bleeding, your wrist is cramping, and you have to stare intensely at the fretboard just to force your fingers into the awkward shape of a C chord.

35:48.780 --> 35:49.220
It's awful.

35:49.220 --> 35:52.900
It is painful, salient, system to labor.

35:53.600 --> 36:02.920
And you also vividly remember years later when you were sitting around a campfire, effortlessly strumming along to a song while drinking a beer and holding a conversation.

36:03.220 --> 36:03.960
That's the best part.

36:03.960 --> 36:07.960
It feels like pure, effortless, system-one magic.

36:08.600 --> 36:19.960
But your brain totally ignores and forgets the 998 intermediate, incredibly boring, mundane practice sessions in the middle where you incrementally improved.

36:19.960 --> 36:28.300
And what is so insidious about this memory bias toward salience is how it creates a false dichotomy in our own self-perception.

36:29.100 --> 36:29.940
A false dichotomy.

36:30.840 --> 36:39.260
By only naming and remembering the two extreme ends of the practice curve, we give ourselves the profound impression that we possess two distinct machines.

36:40.020 --> 36:42.780
The halting, painful start is labeled System 2.

36:43.340 --> 36:45.760
The effortless mastery is labeled System 1.

36:46.040 --> 36:47.540
But the middle part just vanishes.

36:47.540 --> 36:55.700
Right. But the intermediate points, the thousands of tiny mathematical improvements along that continuous slope, they absolutely existed.

36:56.200 --> 36:58.300
They were just unremarkable.

36:58.660 --> 36:59.860
So we threw the data away.

37:00.440 --> 37:06.500
We are constantly confusing the endpoints of a single continuum with a categorical difference in architecture.

37:06.820 --> 37:09.480
Here's where it gets really interesting for anyone listening.

37:09.920 --> 37:13.700
Think about how often you fall for this illusion of salience in your daily life.

37:13.800 --> 37:14.380
Oh, all the time.

37:14.380 --> 37:22.000
Have you ever looked at someone who is incredibly good at their job or maybe an amazing athlete and thought, wow, they are just a natural.

37:22.360 --> 37:24.740
They have such intuitive System 1 talent.

37:25.000 --> 37:25.740
We say that a lot.

37:26.240 --> 37:31.060
We assume they are operating on some effortless, gifted, cognitive machine that we don't possess.

37:31.620 --> 37:34.920
But what you are actually doing is falling for the salience error.

37:35.140 --> 37:35.300
Yep.

37:35.300 --> 37:50.220
You are seeing their 10,000th repetition and assuming it has a different biological origin simply because you completely missed the thousands of hours of grinding, deliberate, agonizingly slow practice they put in behind closed doors to get there.

37:50.460 --> 37:53.820
It completely changes how you view the concept of talent.

37:53.820 --> 37:59.980
It places the emphasis back on the history of the agent rather than magical, innate categories.

38:00.760 --> 38:00.880
Right.

38:01.200 --> 38:07.560
And that brings us to the most technical, but perhaps the most damning of the three illusions, the error of inference.

38:08.080 --> 38:09.460
The error of inference.

38:10.080 --> 38:14.760
This is where we have to look closely at the math of the histogram, which the text formalizes deeply.

38:15.380 --> 38:16.120
Okay, graphs.

38:16.120 --> 38:20.580
In cognitive science, researchers very frequently measure response times.

38:21.160 --> 38:22.940
How fast did the subject press the button?

38:23.740 --> 38:30.700
And very frequently, if you plot hundreds of those response times on a bar graph, you get what is called a bimodal distribution.

38:31.400 --> 38:31.720
Right.

38:31.860 --> 38:34.340
Bimodal meaning a graph with two big humps.

38:34.520 --> 38:34.720
Yes.

38:34.720 --> 38:42.540
You see a large cluster of really fast times grouped together on the left side of the graph and a large cluster of really slow times grouped together on the right side.

38:42.540 --> 38:47.260
And traditionally, for decades, researchers point to that two-humped graph and proudly say,

38:47.360 --> 38:49.960
Aha! Two humps equals two systems.

38:50.100 --> 38:50.340
They do.

38:50.460 --> 38:54.240
There is the fast machine and the slow machine, perfectly separated by the data.

38:54.380 --> 38:56.980
It seems like incontrovertible visual evidence.

38:57.320 --> 38:57.840
But it's not.

38:58.100 --> 38:58.560
Not at all.

38:59.000 --> 39:05.920
The mathematical derivation of the density function in the paper proves exactly why that assumption is statistically illiterate.

39:06.140 --> 39:06.520
Wow.

39:06.520 --> 39:17.360
The math unequivocally shows that a single, strictly decreasing, continuous learning mechanism can easily and naturally produce a bimodal distribution of response times.

39:17.720 --> 39:20.860
I really need you to explain this because it feels like a paradox.

39:21.380 --> 39:30.200
If the underlying practice curve is a perfectly smooth slope with no jumps, how on earth do you generate two completely distinct, separated humps of data?

39:30.200 --> 39:36.540
It has to do with how the density of practice counts interacts with the mathematical derivative of the cost function.

39:36.920 --> 39:37.820
Okay. Lost me a bit there.

39:38.200 --> 39:41.060
Let me try to translate this algebra into a visual landscape.

39:41.340 --> 39:41.580
Please.

39:42.060 --> 39:45.680
Imagine the continuous practice curve as a giant water slide at a theme park.

39:45.940 --> 39:46.940
A water slide. Got it.

39:47.380 --> 39:53.020
At the very top of the slide, when you have zero practice, the slide is incredibly steep, almost a vertical drop.

39:53.380 --> 39:53.680
Okay.

39:53.680 --> 39:58.960
Because it is so steep, your speed improves dramatically with just a tiny bit of forward motion.

39:59.400 --> 40:03.980
But as you travel further down the slide, as you gain more practice, it slowly flattens out.

40:04.460 --> 40:10.160
At the very bottom of the slide, when you have practiced a task thousands of times, it is almost completely flat.

40:10.400 --> 40:11.740
Because you've hit your physical limit.

40:11.980 --> 40:15.840
Exactly. Your speed barely changes at all, no matter how much more you practice.

40:16.440 --> 40:21.420
Okay. I am picturing a massive sweeping water slide that goes from vertical to totally flat.

40:21.420 --> 40:26.980
Now, imagine a massive population of people randomly distributed all along this slide.

40:27.540 --> 40:29.320
We are taking a snapshot of society.

40:29.880 --> 40:30.140
Okay.

40:30.500 --> 40:36.580
Because the top of the slide is so incredibly steep, people spend very little time in that medium speed zone in the middle.

40:37.400 --> 40:41.960
Gravity, or in this case the rapid rate of early learning, pushes them past the middle quickly.

40:42.400 --> 40:43.740
So nobody hangs out in the middle.

40:44.040 --> 40:44.260
Right.

40:44.800 --> 40:48.220
But because the bottom of the slide is perfectly flat, people bunch up there.

40:48.520 --> 40:49.580
They accumulate endlessly.

40:49.900 --> 40:50.740
Oh, I see it.

40:50.740 --> 40:59.080
So if you take a snapshot of where everyone is on the slide right now, you will see a big cluster of people who are just getting on at the top, the slow hump.

40:59.500 --> 40:59.820
Yes.

41:00.060 --> 41:07.280
And you will see a massive, crowded cluster of people who have reached the flat bottom and are just pooling there, the fast hump.

41:07.500 --> 41:08.940
That's the bimodal distribution.

41:08.940 --> 41:17.900
And there will be very few people in the steep middle section because the mathematics of the curve naturally pushes them out of that zone too fast to be heavily recorded.

41:17.900 --> 41:20.180
It is the exact same single slide.

41:20.260 --> 41:23.500
It is the exact same single continuous process.

41:24.120 --> 41:30.140
But the geometry of the learning curve naturally generates a two-humped histogram in the population data.

41:30.400 --> 41:31.660
That is wild.

41:32.320 --> 41:35.200
Therefore, the core takeaway of the error of inference is this.

41:35.980 --> 41:40.500
A bimodal graph of response times does not identify two systems.

41:40.500 --> 41:46.260
The distributional appearance of the data massively underdetermines the underlying architecture.

41:46.260 --> 41:47.700
That is just incredible.

41:48.580 --> 42:00.120
The primary visual proof that cognitive science has relied on for decades to push the dual process theory, these two-humped graphs, is actually completely compatible with a single system theory.

42:00.500 --> 42:02.400
The math literally predicts it.

42:02.400 --> 42:05.120
We have been misreading our own data.

42:05.480 --> 42:06.060
We really have.

42:07.120 --> 42:14.700
Now, to really prove that this single system amortization concept is structurally sound, the source text does something really modern and fascinating.

42:15.120 --> 42:22.440
It leaves human biology behind entirely and applies this psychological theory to cutting-edge artificial intelligence.

42:22.820 --> 42:25.540
This is a brilliant methodological pivot by the author.

42:26.280 --> 42:27.860
The human brain is a black box.

42:28.660 --> 42:30.720
We cannot easily see its internal mechanisms.

42:31.040 --> 42:31.260
Right.

42:31.260 --> 42:37.440
But by looking at machine learning, we can observe cognitive amortization with absolute structural transparency.

42:38.200 --> 42:40.300
We know exactly what the code is doing.

42:40.500 --> 42:41.080
There's no guessing.

42:41.480 --> 42:41.840
Exactly.

42:42.540 --> 42:49.160
The paper cites a highly relevant 2026 study by Lee and Wang, focusing on what are called typed decision models.

42:49.680 --> 42:51.000
Typed decision models.

42:51.760 --> 42:57.560
These are AI models specifically designed to choose among options, judge conditions, or assign scores.

42:57.560 --> 43:06.380
Interestingly, the authors of that specific AI paper explicitly named their creations System 1 models because they're so fast.

43:06.600 --> 43:10.740
And our source author, Flyxion, vehemently attacks that label.

43:11.100 --> 43:12.520
Strongly disagrees, yes.

43:12.740 --> 43:19.700
And they use the actual observable architecture of the AI to prove exactly why calling it System 1 is fundamentally misleading.

43:19.700 --> 43:24.500
So how does this typed decision AI architecture actually work under the hood?

43:24.880 --> 43:26.200
It relies entirely on caching.

43:26.740 --> 43:28.420
Caching like on a web browser?

43:28.880 --> 43:29.120
Yes.

43:29.500 --> 43:33.340
Which is the purest, most literal form of computational amortization.

43:33.340 --> 43:42.340
The model encodes a specific number of potential options, let's call that number K, into a mathematical vector, and it stores them in a local cache.

43:43.140 --> 43:43.360
Okay.

43:43.520 --> 43:48.840
It pre-computes the heavy computational lifting independently before any user ever types in a prompt.

43:49.440 --> 43:54.640
When a request actually comes in, the model doesn't have to evaluate everything from scratch.

43:54.640 --> 44:00.920
It just references the stored entries in its cache, refines the state based on the prompt, and spits out the answer.

44:01.760 --> 44:11.400
The paper introduces an equation here to track how the computational cost shifts, using the Greek letter rho to represent the exact fraction of options that are already cached.

44:11.640 --> 44:12.660
Right. The rho factor.

44:12.980 --> 44:13.980
Let's make this conceptual.

44:14.520 --> 44:22.060
If rho is zero, meaning absolutely nothing is cached, every single option has to be computed completely fresh when the request arrives.

44:22.060 --> 44:25.500
That is the slow, expensive, heavy mode.

44:26.300 --> 44:36.180
But if rho is one, meaning 100% of the necessary options are already safely stored in the cache, the model only has to do a tiny bit of request-specific sorting.

44:36.700 --> 44:40.300
That is the blindingly fast, seemingly intuitive mode.

44:40.580 --> 44:43.280
And here is the critical structural insight.

44:43.800 --> 44:47.060
The cost shift in this model is perfectly mathematically linear.

44:47.780 --> 44:48.100
Linear.

44:48.100 --> 44:52.040
As rho increases, the cost drops proportionally.

44:52.060 --> 44:53.100
There is no sudden jump.

44:53.200 --> 44:57.000
There is no architectural switch being flipped inside the AI.

44:57.740 --> 44:58.120
Ah.

44:58.120 --> 45:01.040
When the model is slow, rho equals zero.

45:01.540 --> 45:03.580
And when it is fast, rho equals one.

45:04.280 --> 45:06.420
It is the exact same model.

45:06.540 --> 45:07.700
The exact same model.

45:07.920 --> 45:10.080
It is utilizing the exact same neural weights.

45:10.400 --> 45:12.100
It is running the exact same procedure.

45:12.100 --> 45:17.060
And it is producing the exact same outputs in exact arithmetic.

45:17.060 --> 45:20.560
The only thing that changes is the parameter rho.

45:21.020 --> 45:23.220
How much of the work was pre-computed?

45:23.220 --> 45:26.620
This reminds me of doing a massive jigsaw puzzle.

45:26.820 --> 45:27.340
Oh, how so?

45:27.580 --> 45:29.540
Imagine you buy a thousand-piece puzzle.

45:30.100 --> 45:34.760
If you open the box and dump it randomly on the table, it is going to take you hours to solve.

45:34.960 --> 45:37.080
Every piece requires deliberate evaluation.

45:37.360 --> 45:37.560
Right.

45:37.700 --> 45:39.100
That is rho equals zero.

45:39.260 --> 45:39.920
It is slow.

45:39.920 --> 45:52.120
But imagine if, before you opened the box, a friend had secretly gone in and pre-sorted all the edge pieces, grouped all the blue sky pieces together, and literally linked all the major character sections for you.

45:52.140 --> 45:52.740
That's a good friend.

45:53.020 --> 45:53.280
Right.

45:53.800 --> 45:56.620
When you open the box, you just snap the pre-built chunks together.

45:56.940 --> 45:58.900
You finish the puzzle in five minutes.

45:59.320 --> 46:00.440
That is rho equals one.

46:00.700 --> 46:01.820
It is incredibly fast.

46:02.380 --> 46:03.500
A perfect analogy.

46:03.920 --> 46:04.760
But here is the point.

46:04.980 --> 46:09.620
Finishing the puzzle faster doesn't mean you magically turned into a different type of puzzle solver.

46:09.620 --> 46:10.420
You didn't evolve.

46:10.680 --> 46:14.200
You didn't swap your brain out for a magical System 1 intuition brain.

46:14.440 --> 46:16.760
You just had the work pre-computed for you.

46:16.900 --> 46:17.280
Exactly.

46:17.760 --> 46:22.600
The label System 1 implies a category-goal difference in the fundamental mechanism of thought.

46:23.180 --> 46:31.400
But the cached AI architecture mathematically proves that low latency, blazing speed, can come purely from the cache.

46:31.760 --> 46:33.920
From just having done the work already.

46:34.140 --> 46:37.080
Which is a property of cost history, not a separate mechanism.

46:37.080 --> 46:46.480
Calling it a cognitive system, that's a false scientific commitment that the category exists independently of the caching that made it cheap in the first place.

46:46.860 --> 46:54.140
But caching exact answers like a giant lookup table or pre-sorting puzzle pieces is only one way to get faster.

46:54.140 --> 46:54.580
True.

46:54.840 --> 46:57.080
What if the task is novel every time?

46:57.360 --> 47:01.220
A jigsaw puzzle is always the exact same picture, but real life isn't like that.

47:01.700 --> 47:07.160
Can practice build something more complex, more adaptable than just a static lookup table?

47:07.740 --> 47:10.180
Because human beings clearly generalize their skills.

47:10.400 --> 47:10.760
Exactly.

47:11.280 --> 47:12.980
We adapt to new situations constantly.

47:13.400 --> 47:16.820
This is a vital question because it blocks a major potential critique.

47:16.820 --> 47:23.920
Someone could read the first case study about caching and say, okay, so practice is just memorization.

47:24.980 --> 47:27.280
System 1 is just a dumb lookup table.

47:27.800 --> 47:29.160
Right, which isn't true for humans.

47:29.500 --> 47:33.780
But as you noted, human cognition is infinitely more flexible than that.

47:34.280 --> 47:44.900
To address this, the paper cites a second groundbreaking 2026 AI study by Wang et al, focusing on progress-aligned recurrence models, or PAR.

47:44.900 --> 47:46.920
Progress-aligned recurrence.

47:47.180 --> 47:47.340
PAR.

47:48.020 --> 47:51.700
This model proves that practice doesn't just store static answers.

47:52.120 --> 47:55.860
It can actually compile highly adaptable, reusable procedures.

47:56.360 --> 48:02.860
Let's set up the experiment from the text because the performance statistics on this PAR model are absolutely mind-blowing.

48:03.000 --> 48:03.560
They really are.

48:04.020 --> 48:10.240
The researchers took a standard, pre-trained language model and gave it complex, sequential state-tracking tasks.

48:10.240 --> 48:16.800
The specific example they use is a game where you follow a series of items being randomly swapped among five different boxes.

48:17.160 --> 48:17.920
Kind of like a shell game.

48:18.180 --> 48:19.700
Yes, exactly like a shell game.

48:20.340 --> 48:26.240
At the very end of the sequence, the model has to answer which specific item ended up in a specific box.

48:26.680 --> 48:29.560
It requires holding and updating a working memory state.

48:29.820 --> 48:29.940
Right.

48:30.520 --> 48:36.620
The researchers train the model to do this by repeating a block of its neural layers a variable number of times, letting it iterate.

48:36.620 --> 48:39.940
And the extrapolation metrics are astounding.

48:40.340 --> 48:40.920
Tell me about it.

48:41.120 --> 48:45.760
They explicitly trained a small model on sequences that were at most 32 moves long.

48:46.160 --> 48:49.600
It never saw a sequence longer than 32 during its learning phase.

48:49.720 --> 48:49.880
Okay.

48:50.240 --> 49:02.960
But when they tested that model on sequences that were 512 moves long, which is 16 times longer than anything it had ever encountered, it achieved 95.5% full state accuracy.

49:02.960 --> 49:09.260
Wait, really? 16 times longer? That is massive generalization.

49:09.720 --> 49:16.840
To put that achievement in context, the best prior recurrent AI method only hit 55.7% on that same task.

49:17.020 --> 49:17.940
That's a huge leap.

49:18.200 --> 49:27.660
And an explicit chain of thought method, where the model writes out its reasoning step by step, utterly collapsed at 0.8% accuracy on the same backbone.

49:27.880 --> 49:28.960
Less than 1%.

49:28.960 --> 49:36.600
So this PAR model absolutely crushed the traditional methods, but why did it work so incredibly well?

49:36.740 --> 49:42.880
What is the secret structural sauce here that allows a model to generalize so wildly beyond its training?

49:43.360 --> 49:50.960
The author identifies two critical mechanisms that made this possible, and both align perfectly with the theory of single-system amortized deliberation.

49:50.960 --> 49:51.960
Okay.

49:52.240 --> 49:57.800
First, the model rescaled its recurrent state to a fixed norm after every single iteration.

49:58.060 --> 50:03.060
Okay, we need to translate that. What does rescaling to a fixed norm mean in plain English?

50:03.400 --> 50:05.760
Imagine you are walking through a dense forest with a compass.

50:06.100 --> 50:06.920
Okay, I have my compass.

50:07.380 --> 50:14.080
If your compass gets slightly knocked off by a fraction of a degree every time you take a step, after 10 steps you're fine.

50:14.080 --> 50:18.660
But after 500 steps, you are miles away from your destination.

50:19.200 --> 50:20.580
Because the error compounds.

50:20.920 --> 50:21.340
Exactly.

50:22.480 --> 50:31.860
Rescaling to a fixed norm is essentially the AI forcing itself to stop, recalibrate its internal compass, and return to true north after every single step.

50:32.380 --> 50:32.880
Oh, wow.

50:33.680 --> 50:37.940
It prevents the internal mathematical representations from drifting in scale.

50:37.940 --> 50:46.960
The text notes that without this reset mechanism, the model's extrapolation capability crashed from a factor of 16 down to below 2.2.

50:47.380 --> 50:48.300
So it's essential.

50:48.820 --> 50:57.320
By anchoring the norm, the model ensured that the repeated transformation actually computed a stable, indefinitely reusable procedure.

50:57.600 --> 50:58.840
That makes so much sense.

50:59.340 --> 51:01.980
It keeps the thought process from degrading.

51:02.740 --> 51:06.840
The second mechanism was the use of progress-aligned targets during training.

51:06.840 --> 51:09.080
Progress-aligned targets.

51:09.500 --> 51:20.160
Instead of just rewarding the model for getting the final answer right at the very end, each iteration of the model was trained to precisely predict the state after a specific number of operations.

51:20.480 --> 51:24.000
It gave every step a defined, measurable milestone.

51:24.340 --> 51:24.740
Exactly.

51:25.260 --> 51:33.720
It forced the model to learn a modular, discrete skill of updating the state once, rather than trying to memorize a holistic path.

51:33.720 --> 51:37.780
This highlights something really important about how we think.

51:38.220 --> 51:45.880
I noticed that this PAR model is doing all of this incredibly deep, extended computation without externalizing text.

51:46.160 --> 51:46.480
That's right.

51:46.700 --> 51:50.820
It isn't generating a long, chain-of-thought paragraph to talk itself through the problem.

51:50.820 --> 52:00.280
When I am solving a hard problem, I often feel like I need to literally talk to myself out loud or write things down on a whiteboard just to keep track of my thoughts.

52:00.380 --> 52:01.000
Most people do.

52:01.400 --> 52:08.100
But this proves that extended, deep computational reasoning doesn't actually require that externalization.

52:08.600 --> 52:12.000
This is a major point of contention in current AI research.

52:12.260 --> 52:14.720
The explicit chain-of-thought model collapsed.

52:15.200 --> 52:18.520
The PAR model succeeded entirely internally.

52:18.520 --> 52:32.080
The text uses this to emphasize that externalization, writing it down or speaking it out loud, is neither what makes extended computation possible nor what distinguishes it as a higher form of thought.

52:33.360 --> 52:38.420
Practice in this context is the acquisition of internal computational competence.

52:39.060 --> 52:46.640
It is learning a robust procedure that can be seamlessly looped and applied iteratively, far beyond the bounds of the original training data.

52:46.640 --> 52:53.960
It is an integer-valued, variable depth of computation, completely divorced from the need to narrate the process.

52:54.640 --> 53:03.480
And there is a fascinating, almost paradoxical quirk in this experiment that the paper brings up, dealing with what we could call the halting problem.

53:03.800 --> 53:04.040
Yes.

53:04.280 --> 53:12.640
This is perhaps one of the most profound behavioral observations in the entire deep dive, and it has massive implications for human psychology.

53:13.060 --> 53:13.840
What did they find?

53:13.840 --> 53:22.840
The researchers found that the PAR models would calculate perfectly, and answer correctly, right at the matching iteration for the problem length.

53:23.240 --> 53:23.480
Okay.

53:23.780 --> 53:30.380
So if the problem was 100 steps, at step 100, the AI had the perfect answer in its working memory.

53:30.540 --> 53:30.980
Exactly.

53:31.700 --> 53:41.900
But if the model was allowed to keep running beyond that point, if it just kept iterating its procedure after it had already found the answer, its accuracy degraded rapidly.

53:42.620 --> 53:43.100
Wait, what?

53:43.100 --> 53:46.640
It would overwrite its own correct solution with garbage.

53:47.240 --> 53:52.580
So getting the answer and keeping the answer are two entirely different cognitive achievements?

53:53.000 --> 53:53.840
That is wild.

53:54.020 --> 53:54.500
Precisely.

53:55.300 --> 53:59.420
A correct answer does not simply persist by default in a dynamic system.

53:59.720 --> 54:00.020
Wow.

54:00.540 --> 54:05.840
Because of this massive degradation, the AI required a heavily trained stopping rule.

54:06.500 --> 54:09.960
It had to learn a specific halting signal to know when to quit computing.

54:10.380 --> 54:12.280
Did they figure out what rule worked best?

54:12.280 --> 54:21.340
The best rule they found required six out of eight consecutive iterations to confirm completion before the model would finalize its output and stop.

54:21.760 --> 54:21.780
Okay.

54:21.860 --> 54:23.440
So it had to double-check itself, basically.

54:23.980 --> 54:30.340
With that gridget halting rule, it maintained nearly 93% accuracy at 16 times the length.

54:30.340 --> 54:36.240
Without the halting rule, the model would inevitably overcompute and destroy its own perfectly correct answer.

54:36.240 --> 54:43.040
We are absolutely going to come back to that idea of overcomputing in a moment because the human implications of that are staggering.

54:43.200 --> 54:43.640
They really are.

54:44.040 --> 54:47.040
But right now, we have reached a critical juncture in the source material.

54:47.600 --> 54:58.620
We have used formal logical proofs, human psychological history, and cutting-edge transparent AI architectures to completely dismantle the system one and system two binary.

54:58.620 --> 54:59.660
The old model is broken.

55:00.360 --> 55:03.600
We have shown that speed is totally dependent on history.

55:04.080 --> 55:07.820
We have shown that continuous practice curves don't have magic thresholds.

55:08.000 --> 55:13.200
We have shown that single systems can mechanically route themselves and naturally produce two-humped graphs.

55:13.480 --> 55:13.720
Yes.

55:13.720 --> 55:20.780
So if we are throwing out this old, comfortable vocabulary, how should we actually talk about thinking from now on?

55:20.820 --> 55:22.540
If I can't say system one, what do I say?

55:23.220 --> 55:30.760
The author concludes that we need to abandon the simplistic binary entirely and adopt a far more rigorous set of distinguishable dimensions.

55:31.120 --> 55:31.480
Dimensions.

55:32.260 --> 55:32.580
Yes.

55:33.260 --> 55:36.920
We can no longer lazily say a thought is just fast or slow.

55:36.920 --> 55:42.520
We need to evaluate the thought process across three specific continuous families of metrics.

55:43.280 --> 55:46.520
History, execution, and access.

55:47.060 --> 55:50.780
Let's break these dimensional families down one by one, starting with history.

55:51.260 --> 55:53.980
What are the specific dimensions we should be measuring here?

55:54.840 --> 55:56.640
The first dimension of history is straightforward.

55:57.020 --> 55:59.560
The absolute number of prior episodes.

55:59.760 --> 56:00.860
How many times I've done it.

56:00.860 --> 56:07.520
Exactly. How many times, mathematically, has this specific agent encountered this specific task over their lifetime?

56:08.000 --> 56:11.020
This maps to the n variable in our power log equation.

56:11.140 --> 56:11.820
Okay. Got it.

56:12.220 --> 56:16.960
The second dimension is the structural distance to the nearest stored case.

56:17.720 --> 56:25.740
If you encounter a problem that is completely novel, how structurally close is it to something you have successfully solved before?

56:25.960 --> 56:26.840
Okay. That makes sense.

56:26.840 --> 56:33.220
And crucially, does that old stored result carry a logical warrant that actually applies to the new situation?

56:33.520 --> 56:36.200
That is the pantry check from the lasagna analogy.

56:36.400 --> 56:37.580
Exactly. The pantry check.

56:37.760 --> 56:37.960
Okay.

56:38.500 --> 56:42.740
And the third dimension of history deals with this specific form of the competence.

56:43.580 --> 56:44.360
The form.

56:44.820 --> 56:45.100
Yes.

56:45.600 --> 56:51.960
Is the practice skill stored merely as static individual instances, like caching answers in the lookup table?

56:51.960 --> 56:57.860
Or has the agent compiled it into a dynamic, reusable procedure, like the PAR model?

56:58.640 --> 57:03.400
Okay. So history covers how our past shapes the physical architecture of the thought.

57:03.940 --> 57:05.920
The second family is execution.

57:06.400 --> 57:07.820
This one seems a bit more straightforward.

57:08.380 --> 57:08.700
It is.

57:09.100 --> 57:16.440
The parameter for execution is simply the actual measurable depth of computation applied to the present task in the moment.

57:16.440 --> 57:19.440
So just how hard the brain is working right now.

57:19.620 --> 57:19.820
Yes.

57:20.340 --> 57:27.900
How many iterations, how much raw metabolic or computational resource is being actively expended right now to resolve the problem in front of you.

57:28.180 --> 57:34.480
But the third family, access, is where I think this new dimensional vocabulary really shines and proves its worth.

57:34.720 --> 57:35.120
Oh, definitely.

57:35.120 --> 57:43.740
Because this replaces all the messy, highly subjective, incredibly vague talk about consciousness and awareness that plagued the old dual process theory.

57:44.380 --> 57:47.840
The paper breaks access down into three distinct measurable subtypes.

57:48.260 --> 57:52.200
And distinguishing these subtypes is crucial for moving cognitive science forward.

57:52.500 --> 57:53.060
Let's hear them.

57:53.420 --> 57:56.720
The first subtype of access is externalization.

57:57.280 --> 57:59.840
This simply asks an observable question.

57:59.840 --> 58:07.140
Are the intermediate states of the computation written down into a physical context where they can be re-read by the agent?

58:07.840 --> 58:08.860
So like taking notes.

58:09.100 --> 58:09.280
Yes.

58:09.500 --> 58:12.060
This could literally be writing math on a piece of paper.

58:12.300 --> 58:18.360
Or it could be generating an internal phonological verbal monologue that you listen to in your head.

58:18.960 --> 58:20.180
Oh, talking to yourself counts.

58:20.460 --> 58:20.720
Yes.

58:21.260 --> 58:23.980
The second subtype is decodability.

58:24.440 --> 58:25.040
Decodability.

58:25.380 --> 58:25.680
Correct.

58:25.680 --> 58:33.500
Decodability asks, can the intermediate states of the cognitive process be recovered or read by an external trained system?

58:34.100 --> 58:35.680
That sounds like mind reading.

58:36.040 --> 58:46.380
Well, in the AI models we discussed, decodability is guaranteed by the architecture because the training objective mathematically forces the latent states to be readable by linear probes.

58:46.640 --> 58:47.600
Right, because it's software.

58:48.000 --> 58:48.300
Exactly.

58:48.860 --> 58:50.960
In a human brain, decodability is much harder.

58:50.960 --> 58:58.920
It might refer to whether a highly advanced fMRI machine could theoretically track the physical progression of the neural state over time.

58:59.180 --> 58:59.980
That is fascinating.

59:00.340 --> 59:04.200
And the third, and perhaps most human subtype, is reporting.

59:04.440 --> 59:04.720
Yes.

59:04.920 --> 59:05.220
Reporting.

59:05.940 --> 59:06.660
What does that entail?

59:07.300 --> 59:12.200
Reporting asks, does the agent actively produce a narrative account of its own internal process?

59:12.600 --> 59:14.500
Basically, can you explain what you just did?

59:14.500 --> 59:14.840
Right.

59:15.160 --> 59:19.040
And the text includes a vital, heavily emphasized caveat here.

59:19.500 --> 59:23.700
The reported account, produced by the human, might not even be accurate.

59:23.780 --> 59:25.120
Oh, we lie to ourselves.

59:25.560 --> 59:30.220
It need not correspond in any meaningful way to what was actually computed mechanically.

59:30.680 --> 59:34.840
We often invent stories to explain our own fast responses after the fact.

59:35.340 --> 59:37.700
This separation of dimensions is brilliant.

59:38.220 --> 59:42.560
It explains so much about human behavior that the old binary system completely failed to capture.

59:42.560 --> 59:44.160
It's a much richer vocabulary.

59:44.880 --> 59:46.460
For instance, think about athletes again.

59:47.080 --> 59:51.140
You can have a thought process that has an incredibly high execution depth.

59:51.560 --> 59:57.180
A quarterback is working really hard, processing the spatial movement of 22 different players in a fraction of a second.

59:57.820 --> 01:00:03.060
But that thought process has absolutely zero externalization and very low reporting.

01:00:03.400 --> 01:00:03.820
Exactly.

01:00:04.500 --> 01:00:09.540
The quarterback cannot explain to you, in words, how he calculated the trajectory of the throw.

01:00:09.720 --> 01:00:10.500
He just threw it.

01:00:10.600 --> 01:00:10.840
Right.

01:00:10.840 --> 01:00:18.960
Under the old System 1 and System 2 model, that kind of incredibly deep but unreportable thought completely broke the categorization.

01:00:19.460 --> 01:00:25.820
The old model lazily assumed that if you were working hard on a problem, you must be consciously aware and able to verbally explain it.

01:00:26.000 --> 01:00:27.220
And we know that's not true.

01:00:27.440 --> 01:00:27.620
Right.

01:00:28.360 --> 01:00:35.680
This new dimensional vocabulary cleanly separates the raw computational depth of the work from your social ability to talk about it.

01:00:35.680 --> 01:00:38.220
The author's summary statement wraps this up perfectly.

01:00:38.500 --> 01:00:39.160
What's the bottom line?

01:00:39.600 --> 01:00:45.820
Cost, automaticity, and reportability do not, by themselves, establish cognitive kinds.

01:00:46.060 --> 01:00:46.320
Okay.

01:00:46.720 --> 01:00:55.300
You cannot point to a fast, silent thought and scientifically claim it is a fundamentally different species of biology than a slow, verbalized one.

01:00:55.680 --> 01:00:56.820
They're all the same biology.

01:00:56.820 --> 01:01:09.540
Explaining the vast variation in human thought requires a full, rigorous account of learning history, internal representation, computational execution, and structural reuse.

01:01:10.000 --> 01:01:10.300
Wow.

01:01:10.300 --> 01:01:23.100
The vocabulary of two systems, if we decide to keep it at all for conversational convenience, should strictly only be used as a casual shorthand for the extreme absolute ends of the continuous practice axis.

01:01:23.200 --> 01:01:23.340
Okay.

01:01:23.460 --> 01:01:25.620
So just shorthand, not a real mechanism.

01:01:25.900 --> 01:01:26.140
Right.

01:01:26.340 --> 01:01:26.500
Yeah.

01:01:26.700 --> 01:01:34.000
It implies no actual mechanism of sorting, and it absolutely unequivocally cannot be read off from speed alone.

01:01:34.000 --> 01:01:38.840
And that profound paradigm shift brings us to the end of this incredible text.

01:01:39.040 --> 01:01:39.660
It's been a journey.

01:01:40.040 --> 01:01:46.660
I want to sincerely thank you, the listener, for coming on this really intensive, demanding intellectual journey with us today.

01:01:46.980 --> 01:01:54.760
The formal logic, the artificial intelligence architectures, the mathematical curves, it is heavy, complex material.

01:01:55.000 --> 01:01:58.140
But it completely changes how you view your own mind.

01:01:58.360 --> 01:01:59.600
It really reframes everything.

01:01:59.600 --> 01:02:08.580
The next time someone at work or a friend at a dinner party claims they just had a system one, intuitive reaction to something, you will know the secret.

01:02:09.240 --> 01:02:13.780
You will know that it wasn't some primitive, separate reptilian brain magically taking over.

01:02:14.000 --> 01:02:14.500
Not at all.

01:02:14.940 --> 01:02:22.340
It was actually just a heavily amortized deliberation, born from their own unique, extensive history of practice and cached evaluations.

01:02:22.780 --> 01:02:26.320
It is an incredibly empowering way to view your own capacity for learning.

01:02:26.620 --> 01:02:27.620
I completely agree.

01:02:27.620 --> 01:02:33.320
You are not trapped by a rigid, dual architecture where some things are forever relegated to a slow brain.

01:02:34.020 --> 01:02:38.360
You are a single, beautifully continuous system, constantly optimizing its own past effort.

01:02:38.600 --> 01:02:39.520
That's a great way to put it.

01:02:40.100 --> 01:02:47.380
But before we sign off, I want to leave you with a final thought to mull over, drawing on something we discussed in the AI recurrence section.

01:02:47.480 --> 01:02:48.540
Oh, the halting problem.

01:02:48.700 --> 01:02:49.100
Yes.

01:02:49.100 --> 01:03:05.560
Remember the halting problem, the fact that the PAR-AI model could perfectly solve a complex problem, but if it didn't possess a rigidly trained stopping rule, it would keep iterating, compute right past the correct answer, and degrade its state into total failure.

01:03:05.560 --> 01:03:12.180
Yes, the realization that getting the answer and keeping the answer are entirely different skills.

01:03:12.540 --> 01:03:16.400
Think about that exact mechanism in the context of your own daily life.

01:03:16.620 --> 01:03:18.140
Oh, I see where this is going.

01:03:18.140 --> 01:03:34.220
How many times have you faced a complex situation, maybe a difficult relationship issue, a strategic career choice, or a creative project, and you actually reasoned your way to a perfectly good, highly functional, correct solution relatively early on?

01:03:34.560 --> 01:03:35.500
And then ruined it.

01:03:35.700 --> 01:03:35.960
Right.

01:03:35.960 --> 01:03:42.060
Because human beings often lack a rigorous cognitive halting rule, you didn't stop computing.

01:03:42.800 --> 01:03:43.580
You kept iterating.

01:03:43.920 --> 01:03:45.260
You kept analyzing the variables.

01:03:45.720 --> 01:03:46.880
The dreaded overthinking.

01:03:47.200 --> 01:03:47.600
Exactly.

01:03:48.180 --> 01:03:51.800
You engaged in what we casually, destructively call overthinking.

01:03:52.360 --> 01:04:04.500
And by continuing to apply deep computational execution to a problem that was already functionally solved, you actually degraded your own perfectly good solution into a state of severe anxiety, paralysis, and confusion.

01:04:04.500 --> 01:04:05.840
That is so true.

01:04:06.620 --> 01:04:14.320
Perhaps the most important advanced cognitive skill we can develop isn't just learning how to deliberate deeply, but learning exactly when to stop.

01:04:15.160 --> 01:04:17.980
What a phenomenal, unsettling thought to end on.

01:04:18.600 --> 01:04:23.860
Remember, keeping an open, rigorously curious mind is the absolute greatest tool you have.

01:04:24.360 --> 01:04:26.180
Thank you for taking the deep dive with us today.

